# Configuration reference

The configuration of the system monitor and of the display is stored in `config.json`, at the root of the project.
JSON has no comments, so this file describes every key of `config.json`. The configuration program (`build/bin/configure`)
writes `config.json` for you: edit it by hand only if you know what you are doing.
`config.json` is created from `config.json.example` the first time a program runs. It is not versioned: changes to the
default configuration go in `config.json.example`.

Keys are grouped in two parts: `config` (settings shared by all the displays) and `displays` (an array with one element per display, holding the settings of that display).

## Section `config`

Settings shared by all the displays.

### HW_SENSORS

Method used to read the hardware sensors:

- `PYTHON`: use the Python-era sensor libraries (psutil, GPUtil...), reading the Linux system files on this version.
  Supports all operating systems but not all hardware.
- `LHM`: use the LibreHardwareMonitor library (Windows only, needs administrator rights). Not supported by the C++ version.
- `STUB` or `STATIC`: use random or static data instead of real hardware sensors.
- `AUTO`: use the best method for your operating system. On Windows, the hardware sensors are not supported yet: `AUTO` uses
  stub sensors (like `STUB`), and `PYTHON` is not available.

Default: `AUTO`

### ETH

Name of the Ethernet network interface, for example `eth0` or `enp2s0`. Leave the field empty (`""`) if the card does
not exist on your setup.

Default: empty

### WLO

Name of the Wi-Fi network interface, for example `wlan0` or `wlp1s0`. Leave the field empty (`""`) if the card does not
exist on your setup.

Default: empty

### CPU_FAN

Fan used as the CPU fan, among all the fan sensors gathered from the motherboard chipset. If the value is `AUTO`, the
system monitor tries to select the CPU fan itself. If the auto-detection fails, indicate the fan manually. The value must
be `controller/fan`, for example `nct6798/fan2`. The configuration program helps you select it.

Default: `AUTO`

### PING

Address used by the ping sensor. It can be an internal or external IP address (for example `8.8.8.8` or `192.168.0.1`)
or a host name (for example `google.com`).

Default if missing: `127.0.0.1`. The configuration program proposes `8.8.8.8`.

### WEATHER_APP_ID

OpenWeatherMap key of the Current weather API 2.5 (free plan, no subscription needed). Create a free account on
https://home.openweathermap.org/users/sign_up and copy the key. Only useful if you use a theme that displays the weather.

Used when `WEATHER_API_KEY` is empty.

Default: empty

### WEATHER_API_KEY

OpenWeatherMap API key of the One Call API 3.0, used to display the weather. You need to subscribe to the 3.0 OneCallAPI,
which has 1000 free daily calls. Only useful if you use a theme that displays the weather.

How the keys are used:
- If `WEATHER_API_KEY` is set, the weather is requested from the One Call API 3.0. A key that is not subscribed to this
  API returns an error, which is displayed in place of the weather description.
- Otherwise, if `WEATHER_APP_ID` is set, the weather is requested from the Current weather API 2.5.
- If both keys are empty, no request is sent and the theme displays `No OpenWeatherMap API key Or app id`.

Limit: with the One Call API 3.0, 1000 calls per day. The program requests the weather every 5 minutes, so about 288
calls per day, whatever the number of displays.

Default: empty

### WEATHER_LATITUDE

Latitude of the place where the weather is displayed. Use for example https://www.latlong.net/ to get it.

Default: empty

### WEATHER_LONGITUDE

Longitude of the place where the weather is displayed. Use for example https://www.latlong.net/ to get it.

Default: empty

### WEATHER_UNITS

Units used to display the temperatures: `metric` (°C), `imperial` (°F) or `standard` (K).

Default: `metric`

### WEATHER_LANGUAGE

Language used by the OpenWeatherMap API. The list of languages is available at
https://openweathermap.org/api/one-call-3#multi

Default: `en`

## Section `displays`

An array with one element per display. Each element holds the settings of one display. The file currently contains one element,
which is used by all the programs. The configuration program edits the first element.

### COM_PORT

Set your COM port, for example `/dev/ttyACM0` on Linux or `COM3` on Windows. Use `AUTO` for COM port auto-discovery (may not work on every
setup) or if the device is not detected as a COM port.

Default: `AUTO`

### THEME

Theme to use. It is the name of a folder of `res/themes`, for example `3.5inchTheme2`. Choose a theme made for your
screen size (see `DISPLAY_SIZE` inside `theme.yaml`).

Required: if missing, the program stops with an error.

### REVISION

Revision of the display:

- `A`: Turing 3.5" and UsbPCMonitor 3.5"/5"
- `B`: XuanFang 3.5" (including flagship)
- `C`: Turing 2.1"/2.8"/5"/8.8"
- `D`: Kipye Qiye Smart Display 3.5"
- `TUR_USB`: Turing hardware revisions 1.x: 4.6"/5.2"/8.0"/8.8"/9.2"
- `WEACT_A`: WeAct Studio Display FS V1 3.5"
- `WEACT_B`: WeAct Studio Display FS V1 0.96"
- `SIMU`: simulated display, the image is written to `screencap.png`. The width and height are taken from the theme.

To identify your screen, see https://github.com/mathoudebine/turing-smart-screen-python/wiki/Hardware-revisions

Required: if missing, the program stops with an error.

### BRIGHTNESS

Brightness of the display, from `0` (completely dark) to `100` (maximum brightness).
Warning: the revision A display can get hot at high brightness.

Required: if missing, the program stops with an error.

### DISPLAY_REVERSE

`true` to reverse the orientation of the display (landscape becomes reverse landscape, portrait becomes reverse portrait).
The basic orientation (portrait or landscape) is defined by the theme you selected.

Default: `false`

### RESET_ON_STARTUP

`true` to reset the display when the program starts, in case it is in a degraded state after a previous run. This makes
the display disconnect and reconnect, and the COM port can change. On some displays (like revision A) this can be a
problem: set it to `false` in that case.

Default: `true`
